.TH CURSES 3
.SH NAME
curses \- screen updating and cursor movement
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <curses.h>

cc ... \-lcurses \-ltermcap
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The curses functions update the screen of a terminal efficiently: a
program writes into
.IR windows ,
arrays of characters in memory, and asks for the screen to be brought up
to date; curses then sends the terminal only what has changed, moving the
cursor by the cheapest means the terminal has. The terminal's codes come
from the termcap data base,
.BR /etc/termcap ,
for the type named by the environment variable TERM, so that the same
program works on every terminal described there.
.PP
A program calls
.B initscr
first, and
.B endwin
before it exits. The window
.B stdscr
is the size of the screen, LINES lines of COLS columns; the functions
without a w at the front work on it.
.B curscr
is what curses believes the screen to hold. Characters are written at a
window's cursor, which moves on; nothing reaches the screen until
.B refresh
(or
.BR wrefresh ,
for another window) is called.
.PP
A character in a window is a
.BR chtype :
the character in its low byte, and attributes in the high byte, or-ed
together: A_STANDOUT, A_REVERSE, A_BOLD, A_UNDERLINE, A_BLINK and A_DIM.
A terminal shows those it can; one with standout only shows reverse and
bold as standout.
.SS "Starting and ending"
.TP 22
.B initscr()
Read the terminal's description, set up stdscr and curscr, and return
stdscr. If TERM names a terminal termcap does not know, or one that
cannot move its cursor, it prints a message and exits.
.TP
.B endwin()
Put the terminal back the way it was, with the cursor at the bottom
left. The next refresh takes the screen over again: the way back after
running a shell.
.TP
.B longname()
The full name of the terminal, from its termcap entry.
.TP
.B baudrate()
Its output speed.
.SS "Terminal modes"
.TP 22
.BR cbreak() ", " nocbreak()
Characters are given to the program as they are typed, or a line at a
time. The default is a line at a time.
.TP
.BR raw() ", " noraw()
As cbreak, and the interrupt, quit and flow control characters are not
special either.
.TP
.BR echo() ", " noecho()
Whether getch and getstr show what is typed in the window. curses does
the echoing itself; the terminal's own echo is always off.
.TP
.BR nl() ", " nonl()
Whether a typed return is turned into a newline.
.TP
.B keypad(win, bf)
With
.I bf
TRUE, getch returns a function key as a single value: KEY_UP, KEY_DOWN,
KEY_LEFT, KEY_RIGHT, KEY_HOME, KEY_BACKSPACE, KEY_NPAGE, KEY_PPAGE,
KEY_DC, KEY_IC, KEY_F(n) and the rest of <curses.h>, as the terminal's
termcap entry describes its keys.
.TP
.B nodelay(win, bf)
getch does not wait: it returns ERR when nothing has been typed. Use it
with cbreak.
.TP
.BR savetty() ", " resetty()
Save the modes, and restore them.
.TP
.BR reset_shell_mode() ", " reset_prog_mode()
The modes the shell had, and the program's.
.TP
.BR erasechar() ", " killchar()
The user's erase and kill characters.
.TP
.B flushinp()
Throw away what has been typed and not read.
.SS "Windows"
.TP 22
.B newwin(lines, cols, y, x)
A new window of the given size, its top left corner at line
.I y
and column
.I x
of the screen. Lines or cols 0 means up to the edge of the screen.
.TP
.B subwin(win, lines, cols, y, x)
A window within
.IR win ,
sharing its characters: a change in one is a change in the other.
.I y
and
.I x
are positions on the screen.
.TP
.B delwin(win)
Free a window.
.TP
.B mvwin(win, y, x)
Move a window to another place on the screen.
.TP
.B touchwin(win)
Count all of the window as changed, so that the next refresh sends all
of it: after another window has covered it.
.TP
.BR overlay(src, dst) ", " overwrite(src, dst)
Copy the characters of
.I src
that are not blank onto
.IR dst ,
where they overlap on the screen; overwrite copies the blanks too.
.TP
.B box(win, vert, hor)
Draw a box around the edge of the window, with
.I vert
for the sides and
.I hor
for the top and bottom (| and \- if they are 0), and + at the corners.
.SS "Output"
Each of these has a form for stdscr, one for another window with a w at
the front, and forms that first move the cursor, with mv at the front:
addch, waddch, mvaddch and mvwaddch.
.TP 22
.B addch(ch)
Write the character
.I ch
at the cursor and move the cursor on, to the next line at the end of
one. A newline clears the rest of the line and goes to the start of the
next; a return goes to the start of the line, a backspace back one
place, and a tab to the next multiple of eight. Other control
characters show as ^X.
.TP
.B addstr(str)
Write a string.
.TP
.B printw(fmt, ...)
Write what
.BR printf (3)
would.
.TP
.BR attron(a) ", " attroff(a) ", " attrset(a)
Turn attributes on or off, or set them, for what is written after.
.B standout()
and
.B standend()
are attrset(A_STANDOUT) and attrset(A_NORMAL).
.TP
.B move(y, x)
Move the cursor.
.B getyx(win, y, x)
(a macro) puts the window's cursor in
.I y
and
.IR x .
.TP
.BR erase() ", " clear()
Blank the window; clear also clears the whole screen at the next refresh,
for when the screen has been spoiled.
.TP
.BR clrtoeol() ", " clrtobot()
Blank from the cursor to the end of the line, or of the window.
.TP
.BR insch(ch) ", " delch()
Insert a character at the cursor, or delete the one there; the rest of
the line moves.
.TP
.BR insertln() ", " deleteln()
Insert a blank line at the cursor's line, or delete it; the lines below
move.
.TP
.BR scroll(win) ", " scrl(n)
Scroll the window up a line, or
.I n
lines (down, if
.I n
is negative). Only a window for which
.B scrollok(win, TRUE)
has been called scrolls; then writing past its last line scrolls it.
.B setscrreg(top, bot)
limits the scrolling to those lines.
.TP
.B inch()
The chtype at the cursor.
.TP
.BR refresh() ", " wrefresh(win)
Bring the screen up to date with the window, and put the cursor at the
window's cursor (unless
.B leaveok(win, TRUE)
was called). wrefresh(curscr) redraws the whole screen.
.TP
.BR wnoutrefresh(win) ", " doupdate()
wrefresh in two parts: wnoutrefresh notes what a window needs, and
doupdate sends it, so that several windows are brought up to date with
one sending.
.TP
.BR beep() ", " flash()
Ring the bell, or flash the screen if the terminal can.
.TP
.B curs_set(vis)
Hide the cursor (0) or show it (1), on a terminal that can.
.SS "Input"
.TP 22
.B getch()
Read a character, after a refresh of the window. With keypad, a function
key comes back as its KEY_ value; the later characters of a key are
waited for a fifth of a second, so that the escape key alone still
works.
.TP
.B ungetch(ch)
Make
.I ch
the next character getch returns.
.TP
.B getstr(str)
Read a line into
.IR str ,
with the erase and kill characters working.
.TP
.B scanw(fmt, ...)
Read a line, and convert it as
.BR scanf (3)
would.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
The functions that return an int return OK (0), or ERR (\-1) when they
cannot do what is asked: a move outside the window, a character written
at the bottom right corner of a window that may not scroll. getch
returns ERR when nodelay is on and nothing has been typed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
	#include <curses.h>

	main()
	{
		initscr();
		cbreak();
		noecho();
		clear();
		move(LINES / 2, (COLS \- 13) / 2);
		standout();
		addstr("hello, world");
		standend();
		mvaddstr(LINES \- 1, 0, "press a key");
		refresh();
		getch();
		endwin();
	}
.fi
.PP
.nf
	cc \-o hello hello.c \-lcurses \-ltermcap
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP 10
.B TERM
the terminal's type
.TP
.B TERMCAP
another termcap file, or the entry itself (see
.BR termcap (3))
.TP
.BR LINES ", " COLUMNS
the size of the screen, instead of termcap's li and co
.SH FILES
.TP 26
/usr/include/curses.h
the definitions
.TP
/usr/lib/cc/libcurses.a
the library
.TP
/etc/termcap
the terminal capability data base
.SH SEE ALSO
termcap(3), printf(3), scanf(3)
.PP
K. C. R. C. Arnold,
.IR "Screen Updating and Cursor Movement Optimization: A Library Package" ,
University of California, Berkeley.
.SH HISTORY
The curses library was written by Ken Arnold at Berkeley, and distributed
with BSD. For System V Release 2 it was much extended, by Mark Horton,
with attributes, function keys and the terminfo data base. This library
has the interface of System V's curses, kept on the termcap data base,
which XENIX uses.
.SH BUGS
printw and scanw use buffers of 512 and 256 characters.
.PP
cc puts printf's floating point conversions into a program only when it
sees a double passed to a function whose name ends in printf, which
printw's does not: see
.BR printf (3)
for the way to include them.
.PP
curses does not use a terminal's ability to insert and delete lines or
characters: what moves on the screen is sent again.
